1
00:00:00,769 --> 00:00:03,938
♫ In the not-too-distant future

2
00:00:03,938 --> 00:00:06,985
♫ Next Sunday AD

3
00:00:06,985 --> 00:00:10,137
♫ There was a guy named Joel

4
00:00:10,137 --> 00:00:13,341
♫ Not too different from you or me

5
00:00:13,341 --> 00:00:16,367
♫ He worked at Gizmonic Institute

6
00:00:16,367 --> 00:00:19,625
♫ Just another face in a red jumpsuit

7
00:00:19,625 --> 00:00:22,466
♫ He did a good job cleaning up the place

8
00:00:22,466 --> 00:00:24,486
♫ But his bosses didn't like him

9
00:00:24,486 --> 00:00:29,010
♫ So they shot him into space

10
00:00:29,010 --> 00:00:32,063
♫ We'll send him cheesy movies

11
00:00:32,063 --> 00:00:35,106
♫ The worst we can find

12
00:00:35,106 --> 00:00:38,259
♫ He'll have to sit and watch them all

13
00:00:38,259 --> 00:00:41,500
♫ And we'll monitor his mind

14
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:44,139
♫ Now keep in mind Joel can't control

15
00:00:44,139 --> 00:00:47,455
♫ Where the movies begin or end

16
00:00:47,455 --> 00:00:50,608
♫ Because he used those special parts

17
00:00:50,608 --> 00:00:54,275
♫ To make his robot friends

18
00:00:55,824 --> 00:00:57,257
♫ Robot Roll Call

19
00:00:57,257 --> 00:00:58,340
♫ Cambot

20
00:00:58,340 --> 00:00:59,973
♫ Gypsy

21
00:00:59,973 --> 00:01:02,108
♫ Tom Servo

22
00:01:02,108 --> 00:01:03,369
♫ Crow

23
00:01:03,369 --> 00:01:06,179
♫ If you're wondering
how he eats and breathes

24
00:01:06,179 --> 00:01:09,347
♫ And other science facts

25
00:01:09,347 --> 00:01:11,485
♫ Then repeat to yourself

26
00:01:11,485 --> 00:01:12,993
♫ It's just a show

27
00:01:12,993 --> 00:01:15,734
♫ I should really just relax

28
00:01:15,734 --> 00:01:19,901
♫ For Mystery Science Theater 3000 ♫

29
00:01:31,949 --> 00:01:34,943
(laughing)

30
00:01:34,943 --> 00:01:36,363
- Oh, what are you guys doing,

31
00:01:36,363 --> 00:01:38,114
you're supposed to be
helping me crunch numbers

32
00:01:38,114 --> 00:01:40,279
for that navigational plot thing.

33
00:01:40,279 --> 00:01:41,648
- I'm not going back, Jim.

34
00:01:41,648 --> 00:01:43,617
- Oh, come on, not this again.

35
00:01:43,617 --> 00:01:45,265
You guys, I've been waiting!

36
00:01:45,265 --> 00:01:46,788
- I'm in love, Jim.

37
00:01:46,788 --> 00:01:49,243
- Hi everybody, welcome
to the satellite of love.

38
00:01:49,243 --> 00:01:50,486
I'm Joel Robertson.

39
00:01:50,486 --> 00:01:51,699
If you haven't already noticed,

40
00:01:51,699 --> 00:01:53,680
my bots Tom Servo and Crow

41
00:01:53,680 --> 00:01:55,533
seem to be obsessing on the

42
00:01:55,533 --> 00:01:57,565
Elias Sandoval episode of Star Trek.

43
00:01:57,565 --> 00:02:00,438
- I like it here on
Omicron Seti three, Jim.

44
00:02:00,438 --> 00:02:03,416
- It really is wonderful
here, Captain Kirk.

45
00:02:03,416 --> 00:02:05,769
- [Voiceover] Five seconds
to commercial sign.

46
00:02:05,769 --> 00:02:07,849
Can I be of any help, Joel.

47
00:02:07,849 --> 00:02:09,478
- No, I just have to get 'em mad,

48
00:02:09,478 --> 00:02:12,174
and then they seem to snap out of it.

49
00:02:12,174 --> 00:02:15,049
You're incapable of feeling love Spock.

50
00:02:15,049 --> 00:02:17,544
- I would not say such
things if I were you, Jim.

51
00:02:17,544 --> 00:02:19,790
- You belong in the circus Spock,

52
00:02:19,790 --> 00:02:22,334
right next to the dog face boy.

53
00:02:22,334 --> 00:02:23,825
- That's enough, Captain.

54
00:02:23,825 --> 00:02:24,658
- We'll be right back.

55
00:02:24,658 --> 00:02:25,491
(laughing)

56
00:02:26,440 --> 00:02:27,545
- Give us a kiss.

57
00:02:27,545 --> 00:02:29,962
('80s music)

58
00:02:33,028 --> 00:02:36,032
- But does she know what
she's getting, Spock?

59
00:02:36,032 --> 00:02:36,951
Your mother was a computer,

60
00:02:36,951 --> 00:02:39,001
and your father was an encyclopedia.

61
00:02:39,001 --> 00:02:42,285
- My mother was a teacher
and my father an ambassador.

62
00:02:42,285 --> 00:02:43,730
- Knock it off, you two!

63
00:02:43,730 --> 00:02:46,397
Ant Dem and Lemon are calling.

64
00:02:47,609 --> 00:02:48,977
- [Voiceover] Now remember,
Hobie, you stay away

65
00:02:48,977 --> 00:02:51,837
from the kids down by the liquor store.

66
00:02:51,837 --> 00:02:52,697
- Hey!

67
00:02:52,697 --> 00:02:54,621
- Baywatch can wait for now, Frank.

68
00:02:54,621 --> 00:02:55,961
With the money we earn
from this week's invention,

69
00:02:55,961 --> 00:02:57,380
we won't have to watch Baywatch.

70
00:02:57,380 --> 00:02:58,760
We'll be able to live it.

71
00:02:58,760 --> 00:03:00,924
- So that means I can be
just like David Hasselhoff.

72
00:03:00,924 --> 00:03:03,111
I'd better practice sucking in my gut!

73
00:03:03,111 --> 00:03:05,544
- You're one to talk, eh Frank.

74
00:03:05,544 --> 00:03:07,083
- Ha ha ha ha ha ha, hey!

75
00:03:07,083 --> 00:03:08,991
- Well, boobie, our invention this week

76
00:03:08,991 --> 00:03:11,975
is based on those new
high-priced TVs that allow you

77
00:03:11,975 --> 00:03:15,622
to check out several other
channels on those small screens,

78
00:03:15,622 --> 00:03:18,492
while you watch one
program on the big screen.

79
00:03:18,492 --> 00:03:20,835
Our invention does that one better.

80
00:03:20,835 --> 00:03:22,462
You see, it actually allows you

81
00:03:22,462 --> 00:03:23,934
to check out and see
what you would be doing

82
00:03:23,934 --> 00:03:25,614
in your life at that moment

83
00:03:25,614 --> 00:03:27,965
if you weren't at home watching TV.

84
00:03:27,965 --> 00:03:29,333
It's called the u-view.

85
00:03:29,333 --> 00:03:30,298
Check it out, Frank.

86
00:03:30,298 --> 00:03:31,624
- [Voiceover] The price is $180

87
00:03:31,624 --> 00:03:34,027
coming to you for only $29.

88
00:03:34,027 --> 00:03:35,768
Quantities are limited.

89
00:03:35,768 --> 00:03:36,601
- Hey!

90
00:03:36,601 --> 00:03:39,696
It's a beautiful, bright, sunny day out.

91
00:03:39,696 --> 00:03:40,949
What's this?

92
00:03:40,949 --> 00:03:43,748
Michele Pfeiffer, Debra
Winger, and Holly Hunter

93
00:03:43,748 --> 00:03:45,478
are motioning for me to come over

94
00:03:45,478 --> 00:03:47,799
and join them in a game of Touch football?

95
00:03:47,799 --> 00:03:48,970
Wow!

96
00:03:48,970 --> 00:03:50,214
They needed a fourth player

97
00:03:50,214 --> 00:03:52,348
and I just happened along, wow!

98
00:03:52,348 --> 00:03:53,936
Hey!

99
00:03:53,936 --> 00:03:56,410
That's Elvis Costello!

100
00:03:56,410 --> 00:03:58,663
He just happened to walk by and he's,

101
00:03:58,663 --> 00:04:00,850
looks like he is asking us
to come over to his house

102
00:04:00,850 --> 00:04:03,059
to hear some new songs he's working on.

103
00:04:03,059 --> 00:04:05,046
Wow, so that's what would have happened

104
00:04:05,046 --> 00:04:06,948
if I had gone out today.

105
00:04:06,948 --> 00:04:08,698
Boy I'm really glad I

106
00:04:09,989 --> 00:04:11,322
stayed inside to

107
00:04:12,539 --> 00:04:13,906
watch

108
00:04:13,906 --> 00:04:14,739
Baywatch.

109
00:04:16,969 --> 00:04:18,469
- Let me try that.

110
00:04:20,202 --> 00:04:24,421
I'm sitting in a Perkins
looking at a menu.

111
00:04:24,421 --> 00:04:27,788
Well that doesn't seem to be
like I'm missing anything.

112
00:04:27,788 --> 00:04:31,684
Whoa, Henry Kissinger and
Richard Nixon are surely

113
00:04:31,684 --> 00:04:33,290
approaching my table.

114
00:04:33,290 --> 00:04:36,907
They are shaking my hands,
patting me on the back.

115
00:04:36,907 --> 00:04:40,627
Henry is giving me a Kissinger
and associates sweatshirt.

116
00:04:40,627 --> 00:04:42,347
Nixon is, what is he doing?

117
00:04:42,347 --> 00:04:44,659
He's pointing at the menu.

118
00:04:44,659 --> 00:04:47,332
Ah, he's recommending the club sandwich.

119
00:04:47,332 --> 00:04:49,499
Oh, I do love this, Frank,

120
00:04:51,892 --> 00:04:56,275
but not as much as I love sitting
at home watching Baywatch.

121
00:04:56,275 --> 00:04:57,570
- [Voiceover] Oh, no!

122
00:04:57,570 --> 00:04:58,514
I'm in big trouble!

123
00:04:58,514 --> 00:04:59,892
I never should have hung out with the kids

124
00:04:59,892 --> 00:05:01,705
down by the liquor store.

125
00:05:01,705 --> 00:05:03,353
- Well, Sirs, our invention may look like

126
00:05:03,353 --> 00:05:04,575
just an ordinary Weber Grill,

127
00:05:04,575 --> 00:05:06,191
but it's much, much more than that.

128
00:05:06,191 --> 00:05:09,197
- Yes, it's the Andrew Lloyd Weber Grill.

129
00:05:09,197 --> 00:05:10,617
- Yeah, everybody, come and get it.

130
00:05:10,617 --> 00:05:14,005
I got a whole ream of
Andrew Lloyd Weber scores,

131
00:05:14,005 --> 00:05:15,632
and they are all ready to barbecue.

132
00:05:15,632 --> 00:05:17,466
- Yes, now you can take
out your aggression on

133
00:05:17,466 --> 00:05:21,201
the theater world's most
inescapable troll-like composer.

134
00:05:21,201 --> 00:05:23,715
- Just stick any of his
many overblown scores

135
00:05:23,715 --> 00:05:25,685
in the Andrew Lloyd Weber Grill,

136
00:05:25,685 --> 00:05:27,302
and they'll burn quickly and efficiently.

137
00:05:27,302 --> 00:05:29,405
- Whether it's Cats or
Phantom of the Opera,

138
00:05:29,405 --> 00:05:31,065
Starlight Express or Evita.

139
00:05:31,065 --> 00:05:33,137
- Hey, wait, I kinda like Evita.

140
00:05:33,137 --> 00:05:34,340
- No time for sentiment, Joel.

141
00:05:34,340 --> 00:05:36,785
We've got to burn Aspects
of Love and Chess.

142
00:05:36,785 --> 00:05:38,462
- Wait a minute!

143
00:05:38,462 --> 00:05:40,328
Andrew Lloyd Weber didn't write Chess.

144
00:05:40,328 --> 00:05:42,815
It was Tim Rice and those guys from ABBA.

145
00:05:42,815 --> 00:05:44,276
- Oh, I think the Andrew Lloyd Weber Grill

146
00:05:44,276 --> 00:05:45,542
can handle them.

147
00:05:45,542 --> 00:05:47,426
And we might as well
bring the score to Annie,

148
00:05:47,426 --> 00:05:49,688
while we are at it.

149
00:05:49,688 --> 00:05:50,663
- Hey, what do you think, Sirs.

150
00:05:50,663 --> 00:05:52,708
(coughing)

151
00:05:52,708 --> 00:05:53,630
- Oh, man!

152
00:05:53,630 --> 00:05:55,006
Elvis is holding up a picture of

153
00:05:55,006 --> 00:05:57,672
Andrew Lloyd Weber and making fun of it.

154
00:05:57,672 --> 00:06:00,626
Holly, Debra, Michelle and
I are having a good laugh

155
00:06:00,626 --> 00:06:02,934
at his expense, we're having a great time.

156
00:06:02,934 --> 00:06:04,594
Why did I stay inside today?

157
00:06:04,594 --> 00:06:06,478
Why, why, why?

158
00:06:06,478 --> 00:06:09,139
Oh, wow, McKenna's Gold is on!

159
00:06:09,139 --> 00:06:11,641
- Well, Joel your experiment this week

160
00:06:11,641 --> 00:06:13,303
will make you wish you were singing duets

161
00:06:13,303 --> 00:06:16,127
with Patty LuPone at
Mandy Patinkin's house.

162
00:06:16,127 --> 00:06:19,274
It's an experiment in
patting from Roger Corman,

163
00:06:19,274 --> 00:06:20,775
and it's called Swamp Diamonds.

164
00:06:20,775 --> 00:06:22,927
But first, a short film called

165
00:06:22,927 --> 00:06:25,172
'What to do on a Date?'

166
00:06:25,172 --> 00:06:27,763
You should be able to relate
to that, will you Frank?

167
00:06:27,763 --> 00:06:29,228
- Shhh.

168
00:06:29,228 --> 00:06:30,894
- Alright, who wants an overture?

169
00:06:30,894 --> 00:06:31,727
- I do.

170
00:06:31,727 --> 00:06:33,375
(alarm blaring)

171
00:06:33,375 --> 00:06:35,708
(screaming)

172
00:06:43,059 --> 00:06:45,309
(coughing)

173
00:06:46,880 --> 00:06:50,346
- I need to know what not to do on a date.

174
00:06:50,346 --> 00:06:52,940
- I should have a PHD in dating.

175
00:06:52,940 --> 00:06:57,107
- No, no, this is like having
your mom tell you about sex.

176
00:06:59,895 --> 00:07:01,037
- These two are on a date?

177
00:07:01,037 --> 00:07:02,703
- No no no no.

178
00:07:02,703 --> 00:07:04,258
- [Voiceover] This is a story
about what to do on a date.

179
00:07:04,258 --> 00:07:05,221
- Goofus and gallant?

180
00:07:05,221 --> 00:07:08,340
- [Voiceover] It begins
one early summer afternoon.

181
00:07:08,340 --> 00:07:09,924
It begins with Jeff.

182
00:07:09,924 --> 00:07:11,341
- Good one, Jeff.

183
00:07:13,322 --> 00:07:14,660
- [Voiceover] And Kay.

184
00:07:14,660 --> 00:07:15,913
- And a human ear.

185
00:07:15,913 --> 00:07:17,005
- [Voiceover] And Nick.

186
00:07:17,005 --> 00:07:18,457
- I meant to do that.

187
00:07:18,457 --> 00:07:20,201
- Bye, Nick!

188
00:07:20,201 --> 00:07:21,701
What a loser.

189
00:07:21,701 --> 00:07:24,385
- [Voiceover] Of course
you may not have a problem

190
00:07:24,385 --> 00:07:25,722
about what to do on a date.

191
00:07:25,722 --> 00:07:29,472
But Nick, well, he has
a real dating problem.

192
00:07:30,307 --> 00:07:32,402
If he took Kay out on a date,

193
00:07:32,402 --> 00:07:33,235
what would they do?

194
00:07:33,235 --> 00:07:34,708
Where would they go?

195
00:07:34,708 --> 00:07:35,541
(glass shattering)

196
00:07:35,541 --> 00:07:36,458
- Dead ass!

197
00:07:37,893 --> 00:07:39,290
- No you can't eject puberty.

198
00:07:39,290 --> 00:07:40,571
It has to happen over time.

199
00:07:40,571 --> 00:07:41,404
- Just a minute, Jeff.

200
00:07:41,404 --> 00:07:42,572
I'll get my tennis racket.

201
00:07:42,572 --> 00:07:43,969
- Okay, Nick, but hurry.

202
00:07:43,969 --> 00:07:48,111
- Now, Jeff, would you quit stalking me?

203
00:07:48,111 --> 00:07:52,111
- Bet he wishes he had a
life, man what a loser!

204
00:07:55,076 --> 00:07:55,953
- My God!

205
00:07:55,953 --> 00:07:59,203
Kay's been missing since last February!

206
00:08:00,420 --> 00:08:02,193
- Hey, what about asking Kay to come

207
00:08:02,193 --> 00:08:03,804
to the community center with us on Friday,

208
00:08:03,804 --> 00:08:06,022
to fix up that scavenger sale.

209
00:08:06,022 --> 00:08:07,062
We can make it a double date.

210
00:08:07,062 --> 00:08:08,559
- You looked in my book, didn't you?

211
00:08:08,559 --> 00:08:10,004
- What!

212
00:08:10,004 --> 00:08:11,925
- You heard what I said.

213
00:08:11,925 --> 00:08:15,087
(mimicking bird calls)

214
00:08:15,087 --> 00:08:17,063
- Take a girl on a date?

215
00:08:17,063 --> 00:08:20,038
Helping to save the scavenger sale?

216
00:08:20,038 --> 00:08:20,871
Is it?

217
00:08:20,871 --> 00:08:22,534
- Why not?

218
00:08:22,534 --> 00:08:24,200
- On a first date?

219
00:08:24,200 --> 00:08:25,829
- First date?

220
00:08:25,829 --> 00:08:28,162
- Yeah, Kay is a swell girl.

221
00:08:29,189 --> 00:08:30,478
(grunting)

222
00:08:30,478 --> 00:08:33,365
I was thinking of calling her.

223
00:08:33,365 --> 00:08:35,335
Maybe ask her to the show Friday.

224
00:08:35,335 --> 00:08:37,443
Good picture, wagon train.

225
00:08:37,443 --> 00:08:38,598
- Well, hurry up and call her.

226
00:08:38,598 --> 00:08:39,618
Let's get going.

227
00:08:39,618 --> 00:08:41,502
I still have to get my things.

228
00:08:41,502 --> 00:08:42,791
- Call her now?

229
00:08:42,791 --> 00:08:43,983
- Sure!

230
00:08:43,983 --> 00:08:46,566
You know the number, don't you?

231
00:08:47,523 --> 00:08:48,356
- Okay.

232
00:08:49,272 --> 00:08:52,024
- But I have a forever plaid audition.

233
00:08:52,024 --> 00:08:55,267
- I'm kidding, I'm
kidding, hang up the phone.

234
00:08:55,267 --> 00:08:57,656
- With each dial, he
comes closer and closer

235
00:08:57,656 --> 00:09:01,586
to the most humiliating
moment of his life.

236
00:09:01,586 --> 00:09:02,513
- What if she says no?

237
00:09:02,513 --> 00:09:03,849
- She will.

238
00:09:03,849 --> 00:09:04,932
- Hello, Kay?

239
00:09:05,914 --> 00:09:06,747
- No.

240
00:09:06,747 --> 00:09:07,884
- This is Nick Baxter.

241
00:09:07,884 --> 00:09:09,087
- No.

242
00:09:09,087 --> 00:09:10,429
- Oh, hello, Nick.

243
00:09:10,429 --> 00:09:11,395
How are you?

244
00:09:11,395 --> 00:09:12,376
- I hurt inside.

245
00:09:12,376 --> 00:09:13,879
- Fine, thanks.

246
00:09:13,879 --> 00:09:16,650
Say, Kay, this Friday,
would you like to see

247
00:09:16,650 --> 00:09:18,582
Wagon Train at the movies?

248
00:09:18,582 --> 00:09:19,536
- I didn't think so, bye.

249
00:09:19,536 --> 00:09:21,254
- Oh, sorry, Nick.

250
00:09:21,254 --> 00:09:24,488
I suppose you've got your
mind made up to see it?

251
00:09:24,488 --> 00:09:26,212
- You've seen it?

252
00:09:26,212 --> 00:09:27,478
She's already seen it.

253
00:09:27,478 --> 00:09:28,319
- Hang up.

254
00:09:28,319 --> 00:09:30,414
- Well ask her about the scavenger sale.

255
00:09:30,414 --> 00:09:31,843
Go ahead.

256
00:09:31,843 --> 00:09:36,278
- Kay, you wouldn't want to
help get the scavenger sale

257
00:09:36,278 --> 00:09:38,988
ready at the community center, would you?

258
00:09:38,988 --> 00:09:40,239
- How many ways can I say no?

259
00:09:40,239 --> 00:09:42,282
- I've been hearing about that.

260
00:09:42,282 --> 00:09:44,113
Yes, I'd like to very much.

261
00:09:44,113 --> 00:09:45,605
- Is it okay if I bring
my boyfriend, Dave?

262
00:09:45,605 --> 00:09:46,605
- You would?

263
00:09:47,585 --> 00:09:48,519
Alright.

264
00:09:48,519 --> 00:09:49,961
I'll club for you then.

265
00:09:49,961 --> 00:09:51,631
